Z||000000||Roy||12-22-1999||07:08 PM||||I was wondering if anyone has ever had there <BR>clutch replaced at pep boys here, I was wondering if the job they did was any good. <BR>Also how tough of a job is it to replace the bearing and seal on the rear right axle? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Tom88gt||12-23-1999||06:19 PM||||No I haven't but since the fieros engine/transaxle appears to be the same as a front wheel drive setup (which is probably 95%) of Pep Boys install jobs they are probably very good at it.  As far as getting it installed right, the clutch alignment tool ($5) takes any guess work out of it.  In other words, its pretty hard to mess it up.  The real trick is pulling the transaxle out of the car, but thats all non-clutch related.  If the price seems fair, get other estimates to, then let them do it.<P>If you are capable of removing the engine, then by all means, try it you self.  You'll be more satisfied, and proud.||||reg||12
Z||000002||DXR_DAD||12-23-1999||10:53 PM||||Hey Roy, That sounds like a topic for FieroShop. Maybe he can answer that, and give a best $$ price on a clutch job?||||reg||5
Z||000003||TOM||12-23-1999||11:37 PM||||This is just my personal opinion. I wouldn't trust pep boys to change a light bulb. But Thats just me||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tom88gt||12-28-1999||01:47 PM||||It's not a question of Pep Boys' skill but the individual people who work at a particular store.  Ask around locally about your particular store and it's mechanics.  Ultimately, you have to decide if you trust these people with your car, if the price is fair or if the job is beyond your abilities.  <P>If you have the time, try it yourself.  It will take some patience but shouldn't be beyond the average fiero owner's ability.  Most auto repairs are very simple straight forward tasks.  Just not always quick.  And make sure you have all the right tools.<BR>||||reg||1
